Expression of genes involved in the control of cell proliferation in developing myocytes is regulated by executioner caspases independently of their catalytic activity {#sec020}
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

To investigate how executioner caspases regulate the expression of the genes found altered by transcriptomics and proteomics in the cardiac-specific executioner caspase knockout's heart, we overexpressed wild type caspase-3 and -7 or caspase mutants bearing a Cysteine to Serine substitution in the catalytic site in P4-5 rat postnatal myocytes ([Fig 5A and 5B](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}), which express low levels of apoptotic genes \[[@pone.0131411.ref024]\], and we assessed the expression of genes affected by *in vivo* caspase deletion. Rat neonatal cardiac myocytes were chose instead of mouse myocytes due to the difficulty in obtaining healthy and abundant cultures of the later and based on the validity of inter-species comparisons reported elsewhere \[[@pone.0131411.ref033]\]. Wild type and mutant caspases were overexpressed at similar levels ([Fig 5C](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}). Caspase proteolytic activity was altered neither in cardiomyocytes overexpressing caspase-3 and -7 nor in hearts deficient for these caspases ([Fig 5D](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}). Increased caspase activity was detected only when the kinase inhibitor staurosporine, an inducer of apoptosis \[[@pone.0131411.ref017]\], was added to the culture medium of the HEK293 cell line or wild type caspase-overexpressing myocytes ([Fig 5D](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}). Lack of caspase activity in normal, non-overexpressing, postnatal myocytes is due to very low expression of these genes, as previously described \[[@pone.0131411.ref024]\].This experiment also confirmed that Cysteine to Serine mutation abolishes caspase proteolytic activity ([Fig 5D](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}). Overexpression of wild type zymogens or the inactive mutants lead to similar increases in the expression of genes downregulated in the caspase knockout myocardium, confirming a direct implication of caspases in the control of genes involved in the regulation of myocyte proliferation ([Fig 5E](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}). We further confirmed enhanced expression at the protein level for cyclin-E ([Fig 5F](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}), which is involved in the proliferation of terminally differentiated myocytes \[[@pone.0131411.ref034]\]. Caspase overexpression also induced a small yet significant downregulation of serpina3 expression ([Fig 5E](#pone.0131411.g005){ref-type="fig"}), a gene upregulated in the caspase knockout hearts, confirming that caspase overexpression induced opposite effects than those observed in caspase-deficient myocytes. Furthermore, observation that changes in gene expression in the caspase-deficient hearts and in cardiomyocytes overexpressing either wild type or mutant caspases occur in the absence of changes in caspase activity demonstrate that gene expression regulation by executioner caspases is independent of caspase proteolytic activity.

